**Answer: Yes.**

**Proof.**

We show something stronger: there exists a connected topological space on which $S_3$ acts *freely* (every non-identity element has no fixed points), which in particular means the action has no fixed points.

**Key fact.** Deck transformations of a connected covering space act freely. Specifically, if $p\colon \widetilde{X}\to X$ is a covering map with $\widetilde{X}$ connected, and $f\colon \widetilde{X}\to\widetilde{X}$ is a deck transformation (i.e. $p\circ f = p$) satisfying $f(\tilde x_0)=\tilde x_0$ for some $\tilde x_0\in\widetilde X$, then $f=\mathrm{id}_{\widetilde X}$. This follows from the unique lifting theorem: $f$ and $\mathrm{id}$ are both lifts of $p\colon\widetilde X\to X$ agreeing at $\tilde x_0$, so they agree everywhere.

**Construction.** Let $X$ be the wedge of two circles (a figure-eight), so $\pi_1(X)\cong F_2=\langle a,b\rangle$. Define a surjective homomorphism
$$\varphi\colon F_2\longrightarrow S_3,\qquad a\mapsto (12),\quad b\mapsto (123).$$
The images $(12)$ and $(123)$ generate $S_3$ (a transposition together with a 3-cycle generates all of $S_3$), so $\varphi$ is indeed surjective. Let $N=\ker\varphi\trianglelefteq F_2$; then $F_2/N\cong S_3$.

Let $p\colon\widetilde X\to X$ be the covering space corresponding to the normal subgroup $N$. Since $N$ is normal, this is a *regular* (Galois) covering whose deck transformation group is
$$\mathrm{Deck}(\widetilde X/X)\cong F_2/N\cong S_3.$$

**Connectedness.** $\widetilde X$ is connected: it is the quotient of the (connected) universal cover of $X$ by the action of $N$, hence connected. (Equivalently, $N$ has finite index $|S_3|=6$ in $F_2$, and the covering is a connected 6-sheeted cover; concretely, $\widetilde X$ is a finite connected graph—a 6-sheeted cover of the figure-eight.)

**Freeness.** The group $S_3\cong\mathrm{Deck}(\widetilde X/X)$ acts on $\widetilde X$ as deck transformations. By the key fact above, since $\widetilde X$ is connected, this action is free: any deck transformation fixing a point is the identity. Hence every non-identity element of $S_3$ acts without fixed points, and in particular the action has no (global) fixed point.

Thus $\widetilde X$ is a connected topological space (indeed a connected graph) on which $S_3$ acts without fixed points. $\blacksquare$

